          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does antibiotic stewardship guidance say about upper respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "Antibiotic stewardship guidance says that most acute upper respiratory infections are viral, and antibiotics should generally not be used for them. This is the \"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship\" guideline summary. It covers the common cold, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and uncomplicated early sinusitis. The guidance says that avoiding antibiotics is an active recommendation, not just the absence of one. It also says that antibiotics do not shorten these infections, do not prevent complications at a rate that justifies routine use, and can cause harm. The listed harms include side effects, allergic reactions, *C. difficile* infection, and antibiotic resistance.\n\nThe guidance also says that talking with the patient is part of the treatment. Clinicians are advised to explain the expected course, how long symptoms usually last, and what would change the plan, and to offer symptom relief. This comes from the same guideline summary.\n\nThe \"General clinical reference\" summary on the common cold says that supportive care (rest, fluids, and symptom relief) is the usual approach. It also says over-the-counter remedies have modest effects on symptoms and no effect on how long the illness lasts, and several are not suitable for young children.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover specific drugs, doses, or the exact criteria clinicians use to decide when an antibiotic is justified. For personal decisions, talk with a clinician or pharmacist.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
